  "num_turns": null,
  "final_message": "Fixed `session_ttl_expired` in `session_ttl.py`, and `test_public.py` now passes.\n\n**The bug:** `created_iso` was parsed with its timezone kept, but `now_iso` had its timezone removed with `.replace(tzinfo=None)`. Python won't subtract a datetime with a timezone from one without, so any `+00:00` timestamp raised `TypeError: can't subtract offset-naive and offset-aware datetimes`.\n\n**The fix:** I deleted that `.replace(tzinfo=None)` call, so both timestamps are parsed the same way and subtract correctly.\n\nTwo extra spot checks came back right:\n- Timestamps in different zones compare correctly: `00:00+00:00` to `01:00:30+01:00` is 30 seconds, so a 60-second TTL is not expired.\n- Two timestamps without a timezone still work: exactly 60 seconds apart with a 60-second TTL counts as expired.\n\nOne thing I didn't fix: mixing a timestamp that has a timezone with one that doesn't still raises `TypeError`, because the README doesn't say what should happen then. The formatter also flagged a long line that was already there before my change; I left it alone to keep the fix minimal.\n\nDONE: Stopped `session_ttl_expired` from dropping `now_iso`'s timezone, so timestamps like `+00:00` compare correctly and the public test passes.",
