      "elapsed_ms": 33255.36320800893,
      "final_message": "I rewrote `solution.py` so `merge_intervals` follows the README contract, and both public tests pass.\n\nWhat it does now:\n- **Checks input:** anything that isn't a list raises `TypeError`. An interval that doesn't have exactly two items, uses non-int or bool bounds, or has start greater than end raises `ValueError`.\n- **Merges:** it sorts by start. An interval that begins at or before the current merged end joins it, so touching intervals like `[1,2]` and `[2,3]` become `[1,3]`.\n- **Doesn't touch the input:** it builds a fresh list of `[start, end]` pairs, and an empty input gives `[]`.\n\nI also ran a few spot checks outside the test file. They covered tuple inputs, touching and separate intervals, and each kind of invalid input. They all behaved correctly, and the input list came back unchanged.\n\nDONE: Rewrote `merge_intervals` to check input, sort and merge overlapping or touching intervals into a new list without changing the input; public tests pass.",
